I’m a bioinformatician maintaining `seqpipe`, where 64 decompression workers feed one GPU aligner per sample. Since commit `9c7e1b2`, the bounded MPMC queue in `/lib/queue/AtomicRing.hpp` occasionally returns the same FASTQ block twice on ARM64, but never under x86 CI. The queue must remain allocation-free after startup, support 256 producers and 4 consumers, offer wait-free `try_push`/`try_pop` in the uncontended case, and preserve FIFO order per producer; dropping a read is unacceptable.

The relevant code is:

```cpp
bool try_push(Block* p) {
  auto t = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot& s = slots[t & mask];
  if (s.seq.load(std::memory_order_acquire) != t) return false;
  s.ptr.store(p, std::memory_order_relaxed);
  s.seq.store(t + 1, std::memory_order_release);
  tail.compare_exchange_weak(t, t + 1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
  return true;
}
bool try_pop(Block*& out) {
  auto h = head.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot& s = slots[h & mask];
  while (s.seq.load(std::memory_order_acquire) != h + 1) {}
  out = s.ptr.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  s.seq.store(h + capacity, std::memory_order_release);
  return true;
}
```

`Block` is returned to a lock-free freelist immediately after the aligner consumes it; the freelist uses tagged 32-bit indices and wraps. Shutdown can race with producers, and a cancelled sample may destroy its queue while a consumer is spinning. We cannot hide this behind a mutex or raise the queue capacity without explaining memory and latency costs.

Reproduce the failure with `/tests/stress_queue.cpp`, ThreadSanitizer, and an ARM Neoverse runner, then audit the algorithm against C++20 atomics and a precise linearizability history. Explain whether the current API is actually wait-free, locate every ABA/use-after-free/data-race path, and either patch it or justify a replacement using sequence counters, hazard or epoch reclamation, or a bounded ticket design. Preserve the ABI of `Queue<Block*>`, cancellation semantics, and per-producer ordering.

Use these observations as leads, not truth:

```text
arm64-runner-03  sample=SRR918273 worker=17
duplicate block id=0x7fa1 offset=4194304
consumer head=1048575 slot=63 seq=1048640 expected=1048576
TSAN: heap-use-after-free in AlignBatch::release()
x86_64: 3.2e9 ops, 0 failures
```

Deliver the patch, a litmus-test suite for ARM and x86, a bounded model-check or state-space argument for capacity 64, and a failure-safe shutdown/reclamation protocol. Include benchmark results at 256 producers and explain any ordering or progress guarantee you cannot prove.

At `/research/selection`, I need a result we can put in an investment committee note and an implementation we can run in production. We rank 4,096 market-neutral signals using 756 days of overlapping five-day returns, keep the top 64 z-scores, and re-screen monthly for 24 months. The old notebook treats the scores as independent Gaussian draws and reports a 2.1% probability that any selected signal has true mean ≤ −ε; the backtest is visibly overconfident when signals share a common factor. Use the model `r_{i,t}=b_i^T f_t+ξ_{i,t}`, where `f_t` and `ξ_{i,t}` are conditionally sub-Gaussian martingale differences, the factor covariance proxy is known, idiosyncratic variance proxies are estimated online, and selection is constrained by a 64-name cap, sector caps, and a 20% monthly turnover limit.

The current code is deliberately naïve:

```python
def select(z, sector, previous, k=64):
    order = np.argsort(z)[::-1]
    chosen = []
    for i in order:
        if len(chosen) == k:
            break
        if sector_count(chosen, sector[i]) < sector_cap(sector[i]):
            chosen.append(i)
    return np.array(chosen)

def claimed_pvalue(z, sigma):
    return 1.0 - norm.cdf(np.max(z) / sigma)
```

`src/bounds.py` must return a simultaneous upper bound for `P(there exists a selected i at any monthly look: μ_i <= -ε)`, valid after adaptive ranking and turnover filtering. Derive it from a clearly stated filtration; overlapping five-day returns must not be smuggled in as independent observations. If cross-sectional correlation cannot improve the guarantee without an extra assumption, say so and give the sharp bound available from the stated model. Compare a valid uniform bound with the naïve formula and identify the smallest additional assumption that would justify any tighter factor-adjusted result.

Deliver a theorem-quality derivation, an implementation using log-domain tail arithmetic, and a routine that inverts the bound to report the largest certified ε for a requested confidence level. Do not use Monte Carlo or an asymptotic-only argument. Handle σ→0, nearly singular factor covariance, missing days, and the case where no portfolio is feasible. Use `numpy`, `scipy.special.log_ndtr`, and `mpmath` only where needed for validation. Add property tests in `tests/test_bounds.py` against exact enumeration for n≤12 and synthetic equicorrelated Gaussian cases, plus a short note in `docs/selection-bound.md` explaining whether the result controls false discoveries, post-selection regret, or a different event. Do not claim a confidence guarantee from the backtest alone.

I am maintaining `liblfq` and I have a concurrency issue, but the order of operations is not clear for me. The public README promises a lock-free stack, while the scheduler team now asks for wait-free `pop` for up to 64 contenders, no allocation in signal handlers, and same behavior on x86-64 and ARMv9.

The reduced C++20 code is:

```cpp
#include <atomic>

template<class T>
class Stack {
  struct Node { T value; Node* next; };
  std::atomic<Node*> top{nullptr};
public:
  void push(T v) {
    Node* n = new Node{std::move(v), top.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)};
    while (!top.compare_exchange_weak(n->next, n,
        std::memory_order_release, std::memory_order_relaxed)) {}
  }

  std::optional<T> pop() {
    Node* h = top.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
    while (h && !top.compare_exchange_weak(h, h->next,
        std::memory_order_acquire, std::memory_order_relaxed)) {}
    if (!h) return std::nullopt;
    T out = h->value;
    delete h;
    return out;
  }
};
```

On ARM I see rare duplicate values; TSAN sees nothing:

```text
[arm64][worker-3] pop=41, pop=41, owner=worker-7
[stress] 4.8e9 operations, no sanitizer report
[reclaimer] freed node=0x7f31 while another pop still held it
```

Please determine whether the promised wait-free behavior is possible with this API and allocator; if not, propose a bounded design or revise the contract explicitly, rather than silently calling it lock-free. Fix ABA and reclamation with a scheme that cannot allocate or register from a signal handler, preserve the ABI, and stay within 2× live-node memory. Give the linearization points and memory-order argument, add ARM litmus tests, model-checkable histories for push/pop, and a stress harness that checks no lost or duplicated values. A mutex workaround is not acceptable, and the result must explain why every progress guarantee actually holds.

Tenemos un servicio Rust para `/v2/score` que procesa lotes de 256 vectores de 768 dimensiones. En producción, p50 es 0.42 ms pero p99 llega a 3.8 ms con 32 cores; el objetivo es p99 menor de 1.1 ms, sin aumentar RSS más de 10% y manteniendo el mismo resultado en x86 AVX2 y ARM NEON. El código importante está en `src/score/normalize.rs`, `src/score/dot.rs`, `src/cache/shard.rs`, `benches/score.rs` y `cuda/fused.cu`.

```rust
pub fn score(batch: &[f32], w: &[f32]) -> Vec<f32> {
    batch.chunks_exact(768).map(|x| {
        let n = x.iter().map(|v| v * v).sum::<f32>().sqrt();
        dot(x, w) / n.max(1e-12)
    }).collect()
}
```

El perfil de una corrida fija dice:
```text
perf stat: 2.41e9 cycles, 6.8e8 instructions, 19.4% LLC-load-misses
flamegraph: normalize 31%, dot 27%, shard_lookup 18%, allocator 9%
wrk2: 184k req/s, p50=0.43ms, p99=3.79ms, max=21.6ms
```

Necesito un diagnóstico basado en counters y no solamente una lista de optimizaciones. Mida primero con `criterion` y `perf`, separe warm-cache de cold-cache, y explique si conviene cambiar layouts, prefetch, arenas, SIMD manual, o el kernel CUDA. Debe conservarse el tratamiento actual de `NaN`, `-0.0`, subnormales y empates deterministas; una reducción vectorizada que cambia el último bit no es aceptable para este modelo. Entregue un parche pequeño por hipótesis, pruebas de equivalencia bit a bit, y una tabla de p50/p99/ciclos/LLC misses para cargas de 1, 32 y 256 vectores. También indique que resultados no son confiables si el benchmark mezcla afinidad de CPU, frecuencia turbo y asignación NUMA.

I am an accessibility auditor, so the optimization is acceptable only if keyboard focus, live-region ordering, stable node IDs, and Unicode/ARIA semantics remain byte-for-byte equivalent to the reference. Our C++ accessibility-tree service applies 80k-node updates from a browser renderer; `AXTree::ApplyUpdates` is now the p99 bottleneck, and screen-reader clients cannot tolerate dropped or reordered events.

Scope is `src/ax_tree_diff.cc`, `src/ax_event_queue.cc`, `include/ax_node.h`, `bench/ax_bench.cc`, and `tests/aria_live_test.cc`. The hot loop currently looks like:

```cpp
void AXTree::ApplyUpdates(span<const Update> batch) {
  std::vector<Node*> dirty;
  for (const Update& u : batch) {
    Node& n = nodes_[u.id];
    n.attrs = Merge(n.attrs, u.attrs);
    if (n.role == Role::Live || u.changes_text)
      dirty.push_back(&n);
  }
  RebuildOrder(dirty);
  NotifyObservers(dirty);
}
```

Sanitized profile:

```text
bench --nodes 80000 --batch 256 --updates 500/s
p50=2.1ms p95=8.7ms p99=18.4ms p99.9=41.0ms
allocations/update=913  LLC-misses=31.8%  branch-misses=12.4%
perf top: 46% RebuildOrder, 21% MergeAttrs, 14% UTF8Normalize
```

Bring p99 below 4 ms and p99.9 below 8 ms on both Apple M3 and x86-64 AVX2, with a scalar or NEON-safe fallback, while keeping peak RSS under 512 MB and preserving event atomicity for `aria-live` batches. Measure before and after with pinned inputs, adversarial deep trees, repeated IDs, and 4× burst load; separate algorithmic wins from compiler flags. Consider SoA/cache layout, allocation elimination, SIMD, and whether a GPU path is actually compatible with tail latency, but do not trade correctness for a benchmark score.

Please return the patch, a reproducible benchmark and perf-counter script, proof or tests for semantic equivalence, and a short explanation of any memory-order or observer-notification changes. Accessibility regressions must fail CI, including focus traversal, hidden-subtree filtering, bidi text normalization, and live-region ordering.

Necesito reparar el restore path de nuestro runtime OCI rootless; el fallo es reproducible solamente con Linux 6.8, cgroup v2, overlayfs sobre ext4, y un host con CPU hotplug. Después de restaurar un container congelado con CRIU, en 1 de 200 runs un proceso queda en estado `D` y otro corre en un CPU eliminado de `cpuset.cpus.effective`. No podemos resolverlo dando `CAP_SYS_ADMIN` ni reiniciando el pod: rootless y availability son parte del contrato.

Por favor inspecciona `crates/runtime/src/supervisor.rs`, `crates/runtime/src/pidfd.rs`, `storage/overlay.rs`, `kernel/bpf/exec_guard.bpf.c`, y `tests/restore_stress.rs`. El supervisor usa pidfds y un poller `io_uring`; el programa BPF observa `sched_switch` y `cgroup_skb/egress`; los snapshots overlay usan un idmapped mount. El código relevante es:

```c
SEC("tracepoint/sched/sched_switch")
int guard(struct trace_event_raw_sched_switch *ctx) {
    __u64 cgid = bpf_get_current_cgroup_id();
    __u32 *mask = bpf_map_lookup_elem(&allowed_cpus, &cgid);
    if (mask && !(*mask & (1u << bpf_get_smp_processor_id())))
        bpf_map_update_elem(&violations, &cgid, &ctx->next_pid, BPF_ANY);
    return 0;
}
```

Log:
```text
restore id=7812 cgroup=/user.slice/x cpuset old=0-7 new=8-15
bpf cpu=9 allowed_mask=0x000000ff event=violation
pidfd=47 POLLIN after pid namespace exit; io_uring inflight=3
overlay: whiteout uid=65534 cannot map into userns 100000:65536
```

La corrección debe mantener la invariants: ningún task puede ejecutar fuera del `cpuset.cpus.effective`, incluso durante hotplug y cuando el mapa BPF está atrasado; si el hook solo observa, cambia la arquitectura o identifica el primitive del kernel que realmente hace enforcement. También debe cancelar polls y pidfds sin use-after-close cuando termina el namespace, preservar whiteouts y ownership con el idmap, y no permitir que una restauración parcialmente fallida exponga el filesystem viejo. Añade pruebas de crash injection, 1,000 restores concurrentes, cambios de CPU durante freeze/thaw, y memory pressure. Necesito que explica la carrera exacta, las garantías que son imposibles desde eBPF, y el parche con métricas para demostrar que no degrada el scheduler.

I am the firmware engineer responsible for the BLE bootloader on a battery-powered medical sensor. Review the secure-update path in `boot/ota.c`, `crypto/verify.c`, `storage/slot_map.c`, and `tests/fuzz_ota.c`; assume an attacker can send arbitrary fragments, observe response timing, replay old images, and glitch reset or power, but cannot read protected flash or obtain the device root key.

The critical code currently looks like this:
```c
bool ota_check(const uint8_t *pkt, size_t n) {
    uint32_t len = le32(pkt);
    const uint8_t *sig = pkt + 4 + len;
    if (n < len + 4 + 64) return false;
    sha256(pkt + 4, len, digest);
    if (ecdsa_verify(pubkey, digest, sig) != 0) return false;
    return memcmp(pkt + 4 + len + 64, expected_mac, 16) == 0;
}
```

A field trace shows a reset loop after a malformed fragment and inconsistent rollback state:
```text
[ble] fragment offset=4294967280 length=96
[boot] slot=B image_len=0xFFFFFFF0
[boot] signature=ok version=17 rollback_counter=16
[wdog] reset pc=0x0801A6C4 elapsed=184 ms
[boot] selected slot=B
```

Give an exploitability assessment with reachable states and attack preconditions, then produce a minimal patch that fixes parsing overflow, authentication ordering, replay/rollback protection, constant-time comparisons, key separation, and power-loss atomicity. Check whether signature verification, decompression, and error paths leak information through timing, cache behavior, or fault responses; consider the TOCTOU boundary between the BLE receive buffer and flash DMA. Preserve the 200 ms boot-time budget and 48 KiB RAM limit. Add coverage-guided fuzzing for fragmented inputs, fault-injection tests around each commit marker, property tests for slot recovery, and a threat model covering manufacturing keys, debug unlock, downgrade, malicious peripherals, and compromised update servers. Do not assume that ECDSA verification alone proves the image is safe.

